TEST 47208 -- Copper pluggable with supported speeds
================================================================================
Run:      2026-10-09 18:53:22-19:00:31 NZDT (tb470 time; the x230 holds NZ wall time labelled
          UTC, ~2 s behind tb470; the AR4050S clock is NZDT; the stack clock is true UTC)
Bench:    tb470, consoles u0 (x230, DUT), u1 (AR4050S), u5 (stack master); Test Engineer
          terrenceb@terrenceb-dl
Tester:   bench-runner (by hand: tools/tb ckcon -> tools/ckcon.py with its @poll / @sleep
          directives, tools/rcdiff.py, tools/rc2cfg.py; no framework script)
Graded:   tester
Case:     AWPTCM-T47208 (Draft), objective and precondition empty, 1 step (step text and
          expected result empty)
Build:    swi_f awplus_main-20261008-57 (x230v2_28GS-awplus_main-20261008-57.rel), bootloader 6.2.40
          swi_e awplus_main-20261008-57 (AR4050S-5G)
          stk_a awplus_main-20261008-57, members 1/3/4
Configs:  swi_f.cfg, swi_e.cfg, stk_a.cfg (running-config before each device's first change;
          saved to startup: no)

VERDICT: PASS -- every copper SFP in the x230 links at full duplex at every speed this bench can
         drive it at, with no error from the x230
      AT-SPTXc port1.0.5 vs tb470 eth3 ........ PASS  (auto 100, auto 10, auto 1000, fixed 1000: all
                                                       connected full)
      AT-SPTXa port1.0.1 vs AR4050S port1.0.2 .. PASS  (auto 100, auto 10, auto 1000, fixed 1000: all
                                                       connected full; LLDP sees the AR4050S)
      AT-SPTX  port1.0.3 vs stack port1.0.17 ... PASS  (auto 100, auto 1000, fixed 1000, and forced
                                                       100/full on both ends: connected full)
      AT-SPTX  port1.0.2 vs stack port1.0.2 .... PASS  (auto 100, auto 1000, fixed 1000: connected full)
      AT-SPTX at 10 Mb/s ...................... NOT MEASURABLE (both SPTX ports face IE520 AT-SPTXa
                                                       ports, which reject 10 Mb/s)
CAVEAT:  AT-SPTX at 10 Mb/s was not proven. Both SPTX ports face IE520 AT-SPTXa ports, and the
         IE520 refuses 10 Mb/s ("% port1.0.17: Unsupported speed/duplex combination port1.0.17"),
         so `speed auto 10` on the x230 SPTX ports left them notconnect; this run cannot say how
         an SPTX behaves at 10 Mb/s against a 10-capable partner. A forced (no autoneg) speed was
         run only at 100/full on port1.0.3; the stack port1.0.2 end is an aggregator member and
         refused speed changes.

